# Hard-Constrained Fourier-Induced Gated Singular-Feature Physics Informed Neural Networks for Spatiotemporal Fractional Nonlocal Heat Diffusion Equations

# Abstract
Spatiotemporal fractional nonlocal heat diffusion equations pose substantial numerical challenges because of initial weak singularities, boundary low-regularity structures, and complex internal variations. Existing fractional physics-informed neural networks (fPINN) employ numerical discretization to approximate nonlocal operators. However, these methods remain affected by spectral bias and have limited capability to jointly represent nonsmooth boundary responses and complex interior features. In addition, the soft enforcement of initial/boundary conditions may introduce constraint violations that influence the approximation throughout the computational domain. To address these limitations, we propose a hard-constrained Fourier-induced gated singular-feature fPINN (HFGS-fPINN). By integrating Fourier feature mapping, gated singular-feature embedding, and hard enforcement of initial/boundary conditions, the proposed method captures components at different frequencies, enhances the representation of initial/boundary singular structures, and avoids initial/boundary constraint violations while mitigating their influence on the interior approximation. Numerical experiments in one-dimensional, two-dimensional, and three-dimensional cases demonstrate that HFGS-fPINN achieves consistently higher accuracy than the baseline models, particularly for problems with pronounced singular structures and complex internal variations.
